<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Janaka</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Janaka is the name of the kings of Videha in Vedic and epic tradition. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brihadaranyaka-upanishad">Brihadaranyaka Upanishad</a> King Janaka hosts philosophical contests between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmin</a> teachers, above all the sage <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yajnavalkya">Yajnavalkya</a>, and is praised as a seeker of knowledge. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramayana">Ramayana</a>, Janaka of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithila">Mithila</a> is the father of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sita">Sita</a>, wife of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama">Rama</a>, and he became a model of the wise and detached king.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/janaka.jpg" alt="Janaka" width="320" height="440" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Janaka,_king_of_Mithila_and_father_of_Sita_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Janaka was the title adopted by the kings of Videha, who ruled from their capital, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithila">Mithila</a>, and were descended from King Nimi and his son Mithi, the first king of Videha to bear the title. According to the Vishnu Purana, Nimi founded a city called Jayantpur, later Janakpur, and was cursed to death by the sage Vashishta. As he had no successor, the sages churned his lifeless body, which produced a prince called Mithi, from manthan, churning. He was also called Videha, &quot;the son of the bodiless&quot;, and Janaka, because he was born without a father.</p><p>The best known Janaka in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramayana">Ramayana</a> is Sīradhvaja, son of Hrasvaroman and Kaikasi, who defeated and killed Sudhanvan, king of Samkasya, and gave that kingdom to his brother Kushadhvaja. With his queen Sunayana he found <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sita">Sita</a> while ploughing as part of a yagna and adopted her; Sunayana later bore Urmila.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Janaka held the svayamvara of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sita">Sita</a>, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama">Rama</a> won, and Urmila married Rama's younger brother Lakshmana. Janaka accompanied Bharata to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chitrakoot">Chitrakoot</a> when Bharata went to persuade Rama, Sita and Lakshmana to return to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayodhya">Ayodhya</a>. After Rama's return from exile and crowning as king of Kosala, Janaka became an important figure at his court, and Rama took his advice on many occasions. According to legend he was a devotee of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a> and set up Shivalingas at the four corners of Janakpur.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Late Vedic literature, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shatapatha-brahmana">Shatapatha Brahmana</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brihadaranyaka-upanishad">Brihadaranyaka Upanishad</a>, mentions a King Janaka, dated to about the 8th or 7th century BCE, as a philosopher-king of Videha whose court was an intellectual centre for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmin</a> sages such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yajnavalkya">Yajnavalkya</a>, Uddalaka Aruni and Gargi Vachaknavi. Janakas are revered as examples of non-attachment, and conversations between a Janaka and sages are recorded in the Ashtavakra Gita and the Parashar Gita. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahabharata">Mahabharata</a> he debates the ascetic Sulabha.</p><p>In Buddhism Mahājanaka, a king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithila">Mithila</a>, is a previous birth of the Bodhisatta and the hero of Jātaka No. 539, a tale of perseverance. After shipwreck, a swim of seven days and the winning of the throne, he renounces it and declares that although Mithila burns, nothing of his is burned. The story appears in temple murals in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Janaka” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Rama, son of King Dasaratha of Ayodhya and his first wife Kausalya, wins Sita, daughter of King <b>Janaka</b> of Mithila, by stringing and breaking a bow no one else could string.</blockquote><a href="/work/ramayana">Ramayana · What It Is</a></li><li><blockquote>Witzel identifies the Brihadaranyaka's centre of activity as Videha, whose king <b>Janaka</b> features prominently in it.</blockquote><a href="/work/upanishads">Upanishads · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Brihadaranyaka Upanishad <b>King Janaka</b> hosts contests between Brahmin teachers, and the sage Yajnavalkya expounds the doctrine of the self, atman, and its identity with brahman.</blockquote><a href="/state/videha">Videha</a></li></ul></section>
